  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance of labour migration in ECOWAS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Within the framework of ILO programming, the ILO has for many years supported tripartite dialogue on labour migration and strengthened ECOWAS Commission and ECOWAS member states&#x2019; capacities to govern labour migration within the region and beyond. This support is expected to increase as more resources become available to the ILO for additional projects and programmes in the West African region. It is envisaged that the ILO can significantly increase support to ECOWAS member states in the period 2024-2030 to foster enhanced policy coherence and improve the protection of migrant workers. Significantly, it is also envisaged that with ILO support, ECOWAS member states will strengthen the capacity to negotiate on behalf of their citizens migrating outside the region, this including for labour agreements that also develop and strengthen fair recruitment and protection for migrant workers.
The ILO will continue to work at interregional and intra-regional levels to build political momentum towards stronger and more inclusive multilateral dialogue with RECs, governments and social partners. Improving interregional dialogue and cooperation should serve to strengthen coordinated regulatory frameworks to protect workers at a bilateral (corridor) and interregional level. In bringing together West African countries of origin, the ILO will also work closely with the African Union Commission (AUC) to solicit its convening power, in close collaboration with other development cooperation projects in the region. Furthermore, the research, training and tools developed by the ILO will be designed to feed into dialogue processes to ensure robust and constructive discussion and rights-based decision-making.</narrative>
  </description>
